dwm - dynamic window manager
============================
dwm is an extremely fast, small, and dynamic window manager for X.


Requirements
------------
In order to build dwm you need the Xlib, XInput 2 (libXi) and Lua header files.


Installation
------------
Edit config.mk to match your local setup (dwm is installed into
the /usr/local namespace by default).

Afterwards enter the following command to build and install dwm (if
necessary as root):

    make clean install


Running dwm
-----------
Add the following line to your .xinitrc to start dwm using startx:

    exec dwm

In order to connect dwm to a specific display, make sure that
the DISPLAY environment variable is set correctly, e.g.:

    DISPLAY=foo.bar:1 exec dwm

(This will start dwm on display :1 of the host foo.bar.)

In order to display status info in the bar, you can do something
like this in your .xinitrc:

    while xsetroot -name "`date` `uptime | sed 's/.*,//'`"
    do
    	sleep 1
    done &
    exec dwm


Configuration
-------------
Keybindings and input settings live in ~/.config/dwm/config.lua (or
$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/dwm/config.lua). See config.lua in this repository, which
documents what it can do. dwm reloads it every time the file is saved, no
restart needed. A config that fails to load leaves the running one in place
and shows the error in the bar until it is fixed.

To keep your config in this repository, symlink it:

    mkdir -p ~/.config/dwm
    ln -s "$PWD/config.lua" ~/.config/dwm/config.lua

Everything else (appearance, tags, rules, layouts) and the fallback keys used
when config.lua is missing are set in config.def.h, which is copied to the
generated config.h on build.
